                        content: `# Food Justice and Food Sovereignty

## Introduction
Food justice examines who has access to healthy food and who controls food systems. This lesson explores structural inequalities that create food insecurity and movements advocating for community control.

## Food Apartheid
Food justice advocates prefer "food apartheid" over "food desert" because it acknowledges systemic segregation and active disinvestment rather than natural conditions. Research shows Black neighborhoods have half as many supermarkets as white neighborhoods with similar incomes. USDA identifies 19 million Americans in food deserts.

## Food Sovereignty
Developed by La Via Campesina (1996), food sovereignty is "the right of peoples to healthy and culturally appropriate food produced through ecologically sound methods, and their right to define their own food and agriculture systems." Key principles: self-determination, ecological sustainability, fair livelihoods, cultural appropriateness, local economies.

## Indigenous Food Sovereignty
Colonization disrupted Indigenous food systems through land theft and forced relocation. Indigenous food sovereignty movements reclaim relationships with ancestral foods through projects like the Sioux Chef's Indigenous Food Lab.

## The Right to Food
The International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights (ICESCR) establishes the right to adequate food. Yet 820 million people experience chronic hunger while food systems produce enough for 10 billion.

## References
- La Via Campesina (1996). "Declaration of Food Sovereignty"
- Penniman, L. (2018). "Farming While Black"
- USDA ERS (2021). "Food Access Research Atlas"
- UN ICESCR (1999). "General Comment 12: The Right to Adequate Food"`
