                        content: `# The Midwest's Premium Fish

Yellow perch (Perca flavescens) is beloved in the Great Lakes region.

## Growth Performance

| Parameter | Value |
|-----------|-------|
| Optimal temp | 68-72°F |
| Growth rate | 0.3-0.5%/day |
| Time to 0.5 lb | 14-18 months |
| Time to 1 lb | 24+ months |
| FCR | 1.8-2.5:1 |
| Market size | 0.3-0.5 lb (pan-size) |

## Temperature Response

\`\`\`
Yellow Perch Temperature Response:

80°F+: Severe stress, mortality
78°F: Stress begins
75°F: Acceptable, reduced growth
72°F: ★ OPTIMAL ★
68°F: Excellent growth
65°F: Good growth
60°F: Moderate growth
55°F: Slow growth
50°F: Minimal feeding
45°F: Dormant
\`\`\`

## Why So Challenging?

\`\`\`
1. CANNIBALISM
   - Worst of all species
   - Will eat siblings up to 50% their size
   - Must grade religiously

2. SLOW GROWTH
   - Takes 2+ years for 1 lb
   - Capital tied up long time

3. DIFFICULT BREEDING
   - Require cold period to spawn
   - Delicate egg handling
   - High fry mortality

4. DISEASE SUSCEPTIBILITY
   - Prone to bacterial infections
   - Sensitive to handling stress
\`\`\`

## Successful Production Strategy

| Stage | Duration | Key Actions |
|-------|----------|-------------|
| Fry | 0-2 months | Live feed, 77°F, grade weekly |
| Fingerling | 2-6 months | Train to pellet, grade biweekly |
| Juvenile | 6-14 months | Optimal temp, monthly grading |
| Growout | 14-24 months | Lower density, final size sort |

## Market Opportunity

\`\`\`
Demand in Great Lakes region far exceeds supply:

Wild catch declining → RAS opportunity
Restaurant price: $15-25/plate
Wholesale: $8-12/lb (head-on)
Fingerlings: $0.50-1.00+ each

Niche but lucrative market
\`\`\``
