                        content: `# RAS Design for Barramundi

Barramundi adapt well to intensive recirculating systems.

## System Requirements

| Component | Specification |
|-----------|---------------|
| Tank shape | Round or octagonal preferred |
| Water depth | 4-6 feet optimal |
| Flow rate | 2-4x tank volume/hour |
| Stocking density | 0.5-1.0 lbs/gal (50-100 kg/m³) |
| Oxygen | 6-8 mg/L minimum |
| pH | 7.0-8.5 |
| Salinity | 0-35 ppt (highly adaptable) |

## Schooling Behavior Benefits

\`\`\`
Unlike many fish, barramundi are:

✓ Schooling fish - reduce aggression
✓ Feed cooperatively (feeding frenzy)
✓ Even growth distribution
✓ Lower stress in groups
✓ Can stock densely

Result: Higher yields per volume than many species
\`\`\`

## Tank Design

\`\`\`
Optimal Design Features:

Round/octagonal tanks:
- Self-cleaning (center drain)
- Even water distribution
- No corner stress zones
- 4-6 ft depth

Water flow:
- Tangential inlet
- Center bottom drain
- Good circular current
- Surface aeration

Lighting:
- Moderate (not bright)
- Consistent photoperiod
- Gradual transitions
\`\`\`

## Stocking Strategy

| Fish Size | Density | Notes |
|-----------|---------|-------|
| Fry (<1") | 2,000/m³ | High cannibalism, grade often |
| Fingerling (1-4") | 500-1,000/m³ | Reduce as they grow |
| Juvenile (0.25-0.5 lb) | 50-100/m³ | Stable groups |
| Growout (0.5-3 lbs) | 40-80/m³ | Final harvest density |

## Water Quality Management

\`\`\`
Key Parameters:

Ammonia: <0.5 mg/L TAN
Nitrite: <0.5 mg/L
Nitrate: <200 mg/L (tolerant)
CO2: <20 mg/L
pH: 7.0-8.5
Temperature: 82-86°F (critical!)

Barramundi are fairly tolerant but
performance suffers outside optimal ranges.
\`\`\``
