                        content: `# Choosing Your Species

This decision affects everything - choose carefully.

## Selection Factors

\`\`\`
In order of importance:

1. MARKET DEMAND
   - Who will buy your fish?
   - At what price?
   - What volume?
   - Year-round or seasonal?

2. CLIMATE/TEMPERATURE
   - Your ambient temperatures
   - Heating/cooling costs
   - Seasonal variation

3. WATER QUALITY
   - Source water characteristics
   - pH, hardness, minerals
   - Availability/cost

4. EXPERIENCE LEVEL
   - Your aquaculture background
   - Available mentors/support
   - Risk tolerance

5. INFRASTRUCTURE
   - Existing equipment
   - Space available
   - Retrofit vs. new build

6. REGULATORY
   - Species permitted?
   - Permits required?
   - Inspection requirements?
\`\`\`

## Common Mistakes

| Mistake | Consequence |
|---------|-------------|
| Choosing based on excitement | High-difficulty species, failure |
| Ignoring market | Beautiful fish nobody wants to buy |
| Ignoring climate | Crushing energy bills |
| Starting with multiple species | Complexity overload |
| Copying others blindly | Different markets/conditions |

## Start Simple

\`\`\`
First-time aquaculturists should:

✓ Start with ONE species
✓ Choose a hardy species
✓ Match climate (minimize heating/cooling)
✓ Verify local market demand
✓ Find reliable fingerling source
✓ Connect with experienced growers

Add species complexity only after
mastering the first one!
\`\`\``
