                        id: "l1-3-210",
                        title: "Produce Safety Rule Requirements",
                        type: "text",
                        duration: 25,
                        content: `# Key Requirements of the Produce Safety Rule

If you're covered, these are the major compliance areas.

## 1. Agricultural Water

### Standards
| Water Use | Requirement |
|-----------|-------------|
| Water contacting produce | No detectable generic E. coli per 100ml |
| Harvest/post-harvest | Same as above |
| Handwashing | Must be safe and sanitary |

### Testing Requirements
- Establish water source profile
- Annual testing for surface water (more frequent)
- Annual testing for groundwater
- Keep records of all tests

### Aquaponics Consideration
Recirculating water must be tested - fish don't make water unsafe, but system contamination can.

## 2. Biological Soil Amendments of Animal Origin (BSAAO)

### Treated vs Untreated

| Type | Example | Rule |
|------|---------|------|
| Treated | Composted manure | 4 documented turns, specific time/temp |
| Untreated | Raw manure | Application intervals required |

### Application Intervals (Untreated)
- 120 days before harvest if produce contacts soil
- 90 days before harvest if no soil contact

### Aquaponics Note
Fish waste in recirculating systems is a regulatory gray area - document your practices and consult with FDA or state regulators.

## 3. Worker Training, Health, and Hygiene

### Required Training Topics
- Food safety importance
- Hygiene practices
- Recognizing illness symptoms
- Proper handwashing

### Hygiene Requirements
- Adequate toilet facilities
- Handwashing stations with soap, water, drying
- Signs reminding workers to wash hands
- No sick workers handling produce

### Health Monitoring
Workers must report:
- Diarrhea, vomiting
- Open wounds
- Jaundice
- Diagnosed foodborne illness

## 4. Equipment, Tools, and Buildings

### Sanitation Standards
| Item | Requirement |
|------|-------------|
| Food contact surfaces | Clean and sanitized before use |
| Non-food contact | Clean as needed to prevent contamination |
| Harvest containers | Dedicated to produce, cleaned regularly |
| Storage areas | Protect produce from contamination |

## 5. Domesticated and Wild Animals

### Requirements
- Assess risk from animals
- Take measures to prevent contamination
- Monitor for animal intrusion
- Do not harvest produce contaminated by animals

### In Greenhouses
- Screen doors and vents
- Seal entry points
- Monitor for evidence (droppings)

## 6. Growing, Harvesting, Packing, and Holding

### General Requirements
- Use appropriate sanitation practices
- Inspect produce for contamination
- Handle in ways that protect from contamination
- Maintain appropriate temperatures (if applicable)`
