                        content: `# Strategic Crop Selection

When water is limited, prioritize high-value, water-efficient crops.

## Value Per Water Calculation

| Crop | Yield/sq ft | Price/lb | Water Use | $/Gallon |
|------|------------|----------|-----------|----------|
| Microgreens | 0.25 lb | $20 | 0.1 gal | $50.00 |
| Baby lettuce | 0.30 lb | $8 | 0.5 gal | $4.80 |
| Tomatoes | 3.0 lb | $3 | 2.0 gal | $4.50 |
| Cucumbers | 4.0 lb | $1.50 | 3.0 gal | $2.00 |

**Insight**: High-value specialty crops often justify water investment.

## Water-Efficient CEA Crops

| Crop | Water Needs | Notes |
|------|-------------|-------|
| Microgreens | Very low | Short cycle, high value |
| Herbs (basil, mint) | Low-Medium | Quick turnover |
| Lettuce | Medium | Recirculating efficient |
| Strawberries | Medium | High value offsets |
| Tomatoes | Medium-High | Recirculate possible |

## Seasonal Adjustments

| Season | Strategy |
|--------|----------|
| Summer (high demand) | Focus on low-water crops |
| Winter (low demand) | Can grow thirstier crops |
| Drought periods | Prioritize highest $/gallon |
| Unlimited water | Optimize for yield |

## Succession Planting

\`\`\`
Instead of:
1 crop × full water demand × full season

Try:
Multiple short-cycle crops
- Microgreens (7-14 days)
- Baby lettuce (21-28 days)
- Herbs (28-35 days)

Less time = less cumulative water
\`\`\`

## Crop Rotation for Water

| Rotation | Benefit |
|----------|---------|
| Heavy feeder → Light feeder | Reduces buildup |
| Deep root → Shallow root | Uses different water zones |
| High water → Low water | Allows soil recharge |

**Diversification**: Growing multiple crops provides resilience when one becomes too water-intensive for conditions.`
